# the argument of locke-treatise-2.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Propositional reading of Locke, Second Treatise, sect. 120 (S1 and S2 are only the section heading).
# The "must" in S5 is read as the condition under which the land is held, kept as a plain atom rather than a modal operator.
atom	enters	a man incorporates himself into a commonwealth, entering society with others for the securing and regulating of property
atom	land_exempt	his land, whose property is to be regulated by the laws of the society, is exempt from the jurisdiction of that government
atom	annexes	by uniting himself to the commonwealth he annexes and submits to the community the possessions he has or shall acquire
atom	land_subject	his possessions become subject to the government and dominion of that commonwealth as long as it hath a being
atom	enjoys	someone thenceforth enjoys, by inheritance, purchase, permission or otherwise, any part of the land so annexed
atom	submits	the one who enjoys the land takes it with the condition of submitting to the government of the commonwealth as far forth as any subject of it
P1	S3	enters → ¬land_exempt	kind conceptual
A1	-	¬land_exempt → annexes
C1	S3	enters → annexes	from P1 A1
P2	S4	annexes → land_subject
C2	S4	enters → land_subject	from C1 P2
P3	S5	land_subject ∧ enjoys → submits
C	S5	enters → (enjoys → submits)	from C2 P3
